---
import AtlasLayout from '../../layouts/AtlasLayout.astro';
import VersionLabel from '../../components/VersionLabel.astro';
import {
  CONTENT_STATUSES,
  ENTRY_STATUSES,
  EVIDENCE_CLASSES,
  MATURITY_LEVELS,
} from '../../data/vocabularies';
import { ISSUES_URL } from '../../config';
import { atlasUrl } from '../../lib/entries';
---

<AtlasLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How the AI Evaluation Atlas selects entries, assigns evidence classes and maturity, evaluates sources, represents disagreement, and handles corrections."
  currentPath="methodology/"
>
  <header class="entry-header">
    <p class="kicker">How the Atlas works</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="summary">
      What qualifies for inclusion, how entries are categorized, how sources
      are weighed, and why the Atlas refuses to collapse evidence into a
      single score. <VersionLabel />
    </p>
  </header>

  <aside class="why-matters" aria-label="Why this matters">
    <p class="kicker">Why this matters</p>
    <p>
      A reference work that classifies other people's evidence owes readers
      its own rules. This page is the Atlas holding itself to the standard
      it applies — how entries qualify, how classes are assigned, and how to
      challenge a call. For the spirit behind the rules, see the
      <a href={atlasUrl('editorial-philosophy/')}>editorial philosophy</a>.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <div class="guide-layout" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
    <article class="guide-prose">
      <h2 id="inclusion">Inclusion scope</h2>
      <p>The Atlas includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>evaluation methods;</li>
        <li>benchmarks and suites;</li>
        <li>evaluation infrastructures and harnesses;</li>
        <li>human-evaluation methods;</li>
        <li>automated evaluators, including AI judges;</li>
        <li>red-team and safety-testing methods;</li>
        <li>standards and governance frameworks;</li>
        <li>observatories and monitoring systems;</li>
        <li>audits and assurance approaches;</li>
        <li>documentation artifacts such as model and system cards;</li>
        <li>research concepts relevant to evaluation validity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Inclusion is not endorsement. An entry appears because the practice is
        consequential in how AI systems are evaluated — including practices
        the evidence suggests are weak.
      </p>

      <h2 id="categorization">How entries are categorized</h2>
      <p>
        Every entry is classified along the controlled vocabularies defined in
        the <a href={atlasUrl('taxonomy/')}>taxonomy</a>: entry type,
        evaluation level, lifecycle stage, purpose, evaluator, environment,
        scoring mechanism, and claimed construct. An entry can hold several
        values on most dimensions; the first listed is treated as primary.
        These vocabularies are versioned with the site so that
        reclassification is visible in the change history.
      </p>

      <h2 id="evidence-classes">How evidence classes are assigned</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry receives exactly one of five evidence classes describing
        how formalized and adopted the practice is. The classes are not a
        quality ranking: a formal standard may still be underspecified, and an
        experimental method may be technically strong.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          Object.values(EVIDENCE_CLASSES).map((c) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{c.label}.</strong> {c.description}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Assignment follows the strongest defensible reading of the evidence:
        “formal standard or legal requirement” requires a published standard
        or binding rule; “widely adopted industry practice” requires adoption
        evidence beyond a handful of organizations; anything supported mainly
        by proposals or early studies stays in the emerging or proposed
        classes. Where a practice genuinely spans classes, the entry is
        assigned the class that best describes its dominant use, and the
        overlap is discussed in the entry text.
      </p>

      <h2 id="maturity">How maturity is assigned</h2>
      <ul>
        {
          Object.values(MATURITY_LEVELS).map((m) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{m.label}.</strong> {m.description}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Maturity is about the practice; the evidence class is about its
        formal and institutional standing. “Common but contested” is a
        deliberate category: much of AI evaluation is exactly that.
      </p>

      <h2 id="sources">How sources are evaluated</h2>
      <p>In support of any claim, the Atlas prefers, in order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>the original peer-reviewed paper;</li>
        <li>the official standard or regulation;</li>
        <li>official technical documentation;</li>
        <li>the original benchmark repository;</li>
        <li>official government or institutional reports;</li>
        <li>well-supported preprints;</li>
        <li>secondary analyses.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Company technical reports are clearly labeled as such and treated as a
        separate evidence class from peer-reviewed work. Commercial marketing
        pages are not used as primary evidence for broad claims. Every source
        record carries its own verification status, peer-review status, and
        source type; see any entry’s Sources section.
      </p>

      <h2 id="disagreement">How disagreements are represented</h2>
      <p>
        Where the literature disagrees — on the validity of AI judges, on what
        red-team findings imply about risk, on whether a benchmark measures
        its named construct — entries state the disagreement rather than
        resolving it. The “What it does not establish” section exists partly
        for this: it records the claims that remain contested even when a
        method is widely used.
      </p>

      <h2 id="statuses">Entry status</h2>
      <p>Each entry carries a source-verification status:</p>
      <ul>
        {
          Object.values(ENTRY_STATUSES).map((s) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{s.label}.</strong> {s.description}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>And a content status:</p>
      <ul>
        {
          Object.entries(CONTENT_STATUSES).map(([, s]) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{s.label}.</strong>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every entry records a created date, a last-reviewed date, a reviewer,
        and a source status. Honest incompleteness is preferred to polished
        fabrication: a stub with accurate metadata is more useful than a
        confident paragraph without sources.
      </p>

      <h2 id="no-single-score">Why there is no single score</h2>
      <p>
        The Atlas does not compute a trust score, a quality grade, or a
        composite ranking for entries. Evidence about reliability, validity,
        reproducibility, and adoption answers different questions; collapsing
        them into one number would repeat the exact mistake the Atlas
        documents in AI evaluation itself — treating an aggregate as a
        measurement without establishing what it measures.
      </p>

      <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections, source suggestions, and disputes are welcome via the
        <a href={ISSUES_URL}>issue tracker</a>. A correction that survives
        review updates the entry and its last-reviewed date; material changes
        are noted in the <a href={atlasUrl('changelog/')}>changelog</a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <h2>Contents</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#inclusion">Inclusion scope</a></li>
        <li><a href="#categorization">Categorization</a></li>
        <li><a href="#evidence-classes">Evidence classes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#maturity">Maturity</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sources">Source hierarchy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#disagreement">Disagreement</a></li>
        <li><a href="#statuses">Statuses</a></li>
        <li><a href="#no-single-score">No single score</a></li>
        <li><a href="#corrections">Corrections</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
  </div>
</AtlasLayout>
